-- title:
--   Exhaustion of the moduli package by bounded admissible pieces
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p$, and a ring homomorphism $\iota$ from `Zp2 p` $= W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$ to $W(k)$. Let $\Phi$ be a `FormalODModule` over $W(k)/pW(k)$ (a two-dimensional commutative formal group with a `Zp2 p`-action and a series $\varpi$ satisfying $\varpi\circ\varpi=[p]$), assumed: special for the structure map $\mathrm{Zp2}\,p\to W(k)\to W(k)/p$, meaning its Lie algebra is the direct sum of the complementary invertible submodules `lieZero` and `lieOne`; of height $4$, meaning the kernel algebra of $[p]$ is finite projective of rank $p^4$ at every field-valued point; and with `lieZero` contained in the kernel of the linear part `lieVarpi` of $\varpi$. Let $M$ be a `ModuliPackage` over $W(k)$ satisfying `IsZariskiSheaf`, and let $\eta$ assign to each ring $B$ with a map from $W(k)$, $p$ nilpotent in $B$, and each rigidified triple $t=(X,n,\rho)$ over $B$ an element of $M(B)$, subject to three conditions over Noetherian bases: $\eta$ identifies exactly the `IsIsomorphic` admissible triples, commutes with base change along ring maps on admissible triples, and every element of $M(B)$ is, after passing to a finite cover of $\operatorname{Spec} B$ by basic opens, of the form $\eta(t)$ with $t$ admissible. The conclusion: for every Noetherian $W(k)/p$-algebra $S$ with $p$ nilpotent and every $x \in M(S)$ there are $n_0,m_0$ such that for all $n\ge n_0$, $m\ge m_0$ there exist $f_1,\dots,f_r \in S$ generating the unit ideal and, on each localisation $L$ of $S$ away from $f_i$ (Noetherian, $p$ nilpotent), an admissible rigidified triple $t$ over $L$ with $t.n = n$ exactly, whose both coordinates of the series `act` $(p^{\,n+m})$ of `t.Φbar` lie in the ideal generated by the components of $t.\rho$, and with $\eta(t)$ equal to the image of $x$ in $M(L)$.
--
--   This is the exhaustion step for Drinfeld's moduli problem of special formal $\mathcal{O}$-modules: every point of the moduli package over a Noetherian base lies, Zariski-locally and cofinally in the two parameters, in the bounded piece cut out by requiring the rigidification to have exponent exactly $n$ and its isogeny to divide $[p^{n+m}]$ in the kernel sense (Boutot–Carayol, Lemme 12.3). It is used in the study of the period map, both for the local description of its fibres over affine opens and for its injectivity.
